fix notes -- large-N cross-stream race
======================================
Symptom: kmeans_largeN / kmeans_largeN_assign returned garbage in ~10-30% of
the calls when the input was split into three or more chunks (few unique
labels, many empty clusters, sometimes NaN centroids; a following batch call
in the same process could also come out wrong).

Root cause: the C++ pipeline runs on its own CUDA streams (work[0], work[1],
reduce) while the tensors it reads (x, init_centroids, centroids) were
produced by the caller on the caller's stream (torch's current stream).
Nothing ordered `randn`/`to()`/etc. against the pipeline, so the D2D/H2D
copies could read a tensor before the producer kernel had written it.
Calls with a single chunk happened to be timed such that the producer had
finished; multi-chunk calls changed the timing enough to expose it.

Fix: flash_kmeans/ops.py synchronizes the device before entering either
large-N entry point.  Outputs were already safe (the pipeline synchronizes
its own streams before returning).

Verification: the previous reproducer (N=100000, BLOCK_N=32768, fp32 and
fp16, random init, repeated) went from 1-4 failures per 10 calls to 0/10 in
three consecutive runs; the blob-based equivalence test is now stable.
The race could also be closed inside the C++ layer by importing the caller's
stream and waiting on an event; the Python-side sync is the smaller change
and large-N is a heavyweight call anyway.
